//BUG="a bracketed argument to a core macro panics the compiler when positions are merged"
//OUT="b"

// 10_macros/11_calling_a_core_library_macro.cpl, with one of the types in
// parentheses — which is an ordinary way to write a type
// (12_stdlib/24_a_parenthesised_type_hint.cpl).
//
// `gen_block` (src/compiler/macros.rs) substitutes an argument by giving
// each of its tokens the position of the parameter in the macro body. That
// is what made the plain call in 10_macros/11 work: afterwards every token
// claims to be in src/core/operators.cpl. But it only relabels the top-level
// tokens of the argument. `(char)` is one token, a parenthesis block, and
// the `char` inside it keeps its position in the user's file.
//
// `parse_type` unwraps the parentheses and returns the inner type with that
// position, `parse_function_declaration` adds it to the signature's position
// from the core file, and `FilePosition::add` (src/compiler/error.rs)
// asserts:
//
//     assertion `left == right` failed: cannot join positions in different files
//
// Any block passed to a core macro — `(…)`, `[…]` or `{…}` — carries the
// same mix. The assert is the wrong tool here: joining two positions is
// done to draw a nicer error span, and a span that cannot be drawn is no
// reason to abort a compile that has no error in it.

_def_operator +; _builtin_add; (char); char; char;

fn main
    a = 'a'
    one = char 1
    out "{a + one}"
